2. Infrastructure Explanation

How the Infrastructure Works

1. A user enters www.foobar.com in their browser.
2. DNS resolves the domain name to the IP address of the load balancer.
3. The browser sends an HTTP request to HAProxy.
4. HAProxy distributes the request between the two servers using the Round Robin algorithm.
5. Nginx receives the request and forwards dynamic requests to the application server.
6. The application server executes the application code and communicates with the MySQL Primary database when data needs to be read or modified.
7. The Primary database replicates changes to the Replica database.
8. The server returns the HTTP response through HAProxy to the user’s browser.

Why Are These Additional Elements Being Added?

1. Load Balancer (HAProxy)

HAProxy distributes incoming traffic between the two servers. This improves performance, prevents one server from handling all requests, and allows traffic to continue through the remaining healthy server if one application server becomes unavailable.

2. Second Server

The second server provides redundancy and allows the website to handle more traffic. If one server fails, HAProxy can send requests to the other server, assuming it is healthy.

3. Database Replica

The Replica maintains a copy of the Primary database’s data. It can help with recovery and can serve read queries if the application is configured to use it. Replication is not automatically a complete backup solution.

